_review/FOUNDATION_PROPOSAL.md:27:Keep the decided Core loop condition region, counted-loop bound snapshots and counter advancement. Preserve the restrictions on exits inside operands. Return evaluates its result before leaving scopes. Normal block exit, return, break and continue run the deferred calls of each exited scope, inner scope first and reverse registration order within a scope. Deferred operands are read at cleanup, with dependencies kept alive. If cleanup aborts, stop immediately; no remaining cleanup runs. Explicit abort runs no deferred call and does not unwind. Ordinary locals do not acquire implicit user destructors: explicit consumption, storage teardown and resource destruction remain different operations. Q2.3 fixes sequence destruction in increasing index order, then backing; OrderedMap in ascending key order, key before value, then nodes; Heap payload before cell; Option/Result destroy the present payload. User types follow their checked destroy bodies. Q3.8 explicitly preserves index order for zero-sized elements. Destruction is an ordinary checked call and may itself fail to terminate unless stronger evidence exists.
_review/FOUNDATION_PROPOSAL.md:46:Layout laws: positive power-of-two alignment; size a multiple of aggregate alignment; occupied field bytes in bounds and non-overlapping; required field alignment in native/C modes; unique discriminants; and valid tag decoding. Only if question 5 adopts niches (unused encodings used for variants), require sound niche decoding and eligibility proofs. No niche machinery is required while niches are absent. Zero-size fields have empty byte ranges and may share offsets. Packed mode replaces natural field alignment with explicit unaligned-access premises; it must not falsely prove packed fields naturally aligned. Core uses the hybrid state in C4, with target/layout as a read-only parameter for sizeof/alignof and raw access. Safe typed operations are proved representation-independent where their premises permit it. The concrete algorithm below is one implementation proving these laws, not the premise of every earlier proof.
