_review/FOUNDATION_PROPOSAL.md:56:### C4. Byte memory and value validity
_review/FOUNDATION_PROPOSAL.md:60:**State choice (question 2):** Core and its Lean evaluator use one hybrid state: structured places for safe typed storage, plus byte/provenance blocks for raw and exposed storage. A partial place-to-block/range projection and Rep relate the two. They are not independently writable copies. Safe borrow-view formation retains the structured root/projection and does not itself materialize bytes. Raw address-taking, expose, or passing an address to foreign code materializes the containing storage with a stable block/range mapping; existing materialized storage is reused. sizeof/alignof query layout without materializing storage. Materialization encodes active fields and pointer fragments and selects allowed initialization/byte contents for padding once through C8’s oracle.
_review/FOUNDATION_PROPOSAL.md:68:Block identity is never reused, even if an address is. Deallocation ends all access rights to the old block. Nonzero accesses need provenance, bounds, liveness, alignment (or justified unaligned mode), initialization for reads and write permission for writes. Q3.7’s zero-byte raw access needs only alignment and a non-null address. One-past pointers may be formed but not dereferenced. Address arithmetic cannot silently wrap into another allocation. Integers carry no provenance; `expose` records the block in the address space, and `from_exposed` uses Q3.9's in-bounds-first, then one-past, then no-provenance rule over live, nonempty eligible exposed blocks. Do not add a new arbitrary tie-break where the decided relation permits choices. Pointer equality follows the decided raw model, not equality of allocation identities alone.
_review/FOUNDATION_PROPOSAL.md:70:Zero-size slots own distinct logical obligations, are destroyed in index order (Q3.8), occupy no bytes and may share addresses; address equality does not merge owners. Typed operations still discharge their logical ownership obligations; this does not strengthen the zero-byte raw-access rule. Address collisions mean overlapping occupied bytes, not merely equal bases. Address exhaustion belongs to allocator availability. Allocation failure is a typed result conserving the rejected input, not an implicit trap. Require Q1.11's allocator availability laws: progress under availability, unchanged allocator state on failure, release never reducing availability, and a concrete success witness. Callback effects are not rolled back; every element remains accounted for in its typed result. Raw/foreign writes must re-establish validity before safe typed use; violating a trusted premise yields no safety claim, not a fabricated valid value.
_review/FOUNDATION_PROPOSAL.md:72:**Authority:** Core Q1.8–Q1.12, Q3.7–Q3.11; [memory decision](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-memory-2026-11-06). Hybrid state, initialization/padding and pointer-fragment completions require question 2; they do not revise Q3.7’s exception. **Later:** raw-pointer syntax (24), allocator algorithms (19–23), FFI operations (25). They instantiate this model rather than replace it.
_review/FOUNDATION_PROPOSAL.md:107:**Authority:** [information flow](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-infoflow-2026-11-06), [memory/accounting](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-memory-2026-11-06), Core Q9.10. **Later:** scalar secret rules/proofs at 7, arrays/wipes at 15 and heaps at 19. Binary constant time, hardware timing and actual wiping remain named backend premises until proved; speculative leakage is outside the decided observer model.
_review/FOUNDATION_PROPOSAL.md:111:**Freeze:** both the small-step relation and Q9.9's total fuel-indexed evaluator over instrumented hybrid Core state: `eval(target, layout, environmentChoices, fuel, coreProgram, state)`. This is the reference evaluator for qualification; it executes Core, not BIR or source text. Choices explicitly cover external responses, allocation/address choices, exposed-pointer alternatives and padding materialization. A choice input can be an oracle rather than a finite stream; each answer is checked against the operation contract. Oracle queries are pure deterministic functions of the Core execution history, request kind, logical site and occurrence number, independent of fuel budget; answers are deterministic functions of those queries and the fixed oracle. Materialization queries occur only at C4’s first raw exposure, not on safe borrowing or typed rewrites. Fixed inputs make evaluation deterministic. Native backends need not issue the same queries; P4 checks allowed Core observations instead. Prove evaluator soundness (a reported result has a matching finite execution), completeness (every finite execution is obtained with enough fuel), monotonicity (extra fuel preserves a completed result) and trace-prefix consistency. For nondeterministic effects these statements quantify over the same environment choices.
_review/FOUNDATION_PROPOSAL.md:133:**Rule:** internal calls use an explicit target convention proved compatible with these contracts. At Memory CFG, lower the hybrid Core representation to explicit byte/provenance operations and ABI calls, proving its simulation against C4’s projection. BIR carries the complete resulting contract. Frame accounting binds to the final artifact, including spills, cleanup and launcher/runtime contributions, not merely source locals. Stack facts are separate from heap accounting. Foreign retention needs the decided `kept`/`Kept<H>` contract; FFI-safe types and no-enum restriction remain. Logical frame theorems do not add a source frame clause or snapshot construct.
_review/FOUNDATION_PROPOSAL.md:143:BIR feeds the LLVM printer or the proved BIR→LCB adapter. **Memory CFG stays the single layout/ABI/provenance lowering boundary**; retract P2's folding proposal. Earlier semantics uses C4’s hybrid state and read-only layout interface; representation simulations relate it to this explicit lowering boundary.
_review/FOUNDATION_PROPOSAL.md:163:| OD126; Q3.7/Q3.9/Q3.10 | Generalize address/profile interface, preserving provenance rules and zero-byte exception; existing 64-bit little-endian profile unchanged, including Q3.10’s `base + size ≤ 2^64−1` one-past bound. Any generalized profile needs an analogous checked bound. | 3 |
_review/FOUNDATION_PROPOSAL.md:164:| Q3.5; Q1.11/Q2.4/Q4.6; TYPE-19; FFI-7 | Q3.5 fixes u64 lengths/indices across profiles. Q1.11 rejects counts above u64 as TooLarge; Q2.4 fixes Drained.at: u64; Q4.6 byte offsets/lengths use that index contract. Target-sized usize would revise these and layout-query results. Recommendation retains these semantics and permits only proved narrower representations. | 7 |
_review/FOUNDATION_PROPOSAL.md:171:Q9.11/OD144 already decide post-cleanup ensures timing. The SPARK decision already decides source struct invariants. Neither is a new rule. C4’s hybrid/representation completion is a proposed formalization choice, not authority to revise raw access. Exact trap constructors and byte columns are not inferred from Q6.7 or ArithmeticError. OD106/ARITH-10 already fix conversion errors; Q2.3 and Q3.8 already fix destruction orders. Questions 12 and 13 are removed as fully decided; later question numbers are retained for review tracking.
_review/FOUNDATION_PROPOSAL.md:178:**Q2. Hybrid state and representation completion.** Recommend C4’s structured-place/byte-block state, coherent projection, pointer fragments and explicit initialization/padding laws. Runner-up: use byte blocks for all Core storage. The hybrid keeps typed proofs small while raw operations remain executable; both must preserve Q3.7 and raw observations.
_review/FOUNDATION_PROPOSAL.md:179:**Q3. Target abstraction.** Recommend read-only profiles separating address width, pointer representation, length width, byte order and address spaces; generalize Q3.7/Q3.9/Q3.10 without changing existing profile constants or reconstruction policy. Runner-up: separate semantics per profile under OD126. No usize or enum change is included.
