docs/decisions.md:142:  and foreign-mutation taxonomy and call-only annotation syntax, undecided on 2026-11-03, are provisionally decided by rows Q3.7–Q3.12 and Q9.7 ([rows](#decided-2026-11-05-core)); OD64's confinement
docs/decisions.md:199:- **Q1.8** What if creating allocator state fails? — Fallible allocator construction conserving surviving inputs.
docs/decisions.md:200:- **Q1.9** What do allocation failures contain? — `AllocFailure<T>` returns rejected `T`; operation-specific recovery.
docs/decisions.md:201:- **Q1.10** Are capacity and addresses observable? — Observable capacity; explicit raw-address exposure.
docs/decisions.md:202:- **Q1.11** What does “unchanged” require of allocator state? — Availability predicate plus four laws: progress, unchanged state on failure, release never reduces availability, a concrete success witness.
docs/decisions.md:203:- **Q1.12** What rolls back around callbacks? — No rollback of callback effects; every element accounted for in a typed result.
docs/decisions.md:219:- **Q3.7** What is the raw-memory model and validity taxonomy? — Blocks with provenance; pointer = (prov: Option<BlockId>, addr: u64); integers carry no provenance; every access needs explicit validity premises.
docs/decisions.md:220:- **Q3.8** How do zero-sized elements behave? — Zero-sized slots own their obligations; they occupy no bytes and may share addresses.
docs/decisions.md:221:- **Q3.9** What do pointer equality and integer round trips mean? — `expose` records the block; `from_exposed` picks in-bounds, then one-past, else no provenance.
docs/decisions.md:222:- **Q3.10** What happens when address space runs out? — A collision means overlapping occupied bytes; address exhaustion is part of Avail.
docs/decisions.md:223:- **Q3.11** How are typed values related to raw bytes? — Structured values and raw store joined by `Rep`.
